WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.290
In 2023, a new cargo
ship left the shipyard.

2
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.650
It carries 24,346 twenty-foot containers.

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.760
Ships, factories, supermarkets:
in many industries,

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.170
bigger firms get the
cost of each unit down.

5
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.050
And yet size can also bring problems.

6
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.600
Why can a giant firm
make things more cheaply

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.160
than a small one, and when
does size start to hurt?

8
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.490
In this mission, you'll
sort the ways a firm's

9
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.160
own growth can lower its cost per unit,

10
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.700
starting with two simple cubes.

11
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.970
Then you'll find out
where on the long-run cost

12
00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.180
curve size stops helping.

13
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.220
Your mission: explain
when bigger means cheaper,

14
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.760
and when it does not.
